      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-5{x}^{2}+70.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-10x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-10x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+70}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5x}{\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+70}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-3} & ={(-\\frac{5x}{\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+70}})|}_{x=-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10(-3)}{2\\sqrt{-5(-3{)}^{2}+70}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
