      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+40}$ at the point where $x=-2.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-6{x}^{2}+40.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-12x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-12x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12x}{2\\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+40}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+40}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-2} & ={(-\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+40}})|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12(-2)}{2\\sqrt{-6(-2{)}^{2}+40}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
